**Proposition 17.3 (limits witnessed by this construction).** A fixed graph
without the new activation permission still cannot start this installer
at an arbitrary original busy cut, or take its new Ready edge as though
it were an installed old row. A package cannot change its target by using
a later live meter or latest-cover. Source-only input retention cannot
recover earlier service INs. These restrictions remain after the word
compiler and its native consumer have been constructed.

Proof. The first statement is the unique prescribed-successor obstruction
of Proposition 3.1, and at Ready the original menu still has no Install
edge. Definition 14.1 openly changes this relation and Step explicitly
records its use. For target validation the comparison is with frozen
$b$, so advancing current time past $k>b$ still yields CutIndexReject.
For a successful package $k$ and its input prefix are actual immutable
bytes; later operations append other objects. Lemma 7.2 supplies two
service latch states distinguished by their retained control input but
not by source-only retention. Neither native simulation nor added budget
can reconstruct an omitted choice from those identical old observations. ∎

